8 #edb/ref 17592186403469 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403468 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71407" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403468 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403468 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the surface $z=2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x$ at the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403468 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ca7dad4d-8007-5c21-9019-38d66b5366c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403468 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+x \\\\ z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)=z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$ \n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}-x)k \\\\  & =(-4x-1)\\text{ }i-4y\\text{ }j+k \\\\ ∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3) & =(-4(\\frac{1}{2})-1)\\,i-4(1)\\text{ }j+k \\\\  & =-3\\,i-4\\,j+k \\\\  & =⟨-3,-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ and passes through the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,∇f(\\frac{1}{2},1,3)=⟨\\frac{1}{2},1,3⟩+t\\,⟨-3,-4,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186403469 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186403473 #ed