+ 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(y - 2\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))k\\\\&=\\left(2 x + 2\\right)i+\\left(4 y - 8\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\∇f(0,3,-3)&=\\left(2\\right)i+\\left(4\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\&=⟨2,4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,3,-3)$ and passes through the point $(0,3,-3)$ can be written as\n\n$$r=⟨0,3,-3⟩+t\\,∇f(0,3,-3)=⟨0,3,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,1⟩.$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521982 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521971 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521982 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521982 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the level surface $\\left(-\\left(x - 1\\right)\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 2\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(z - 2\\right)=0$ at the point $(0,-3,5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521982 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6dd1fd09-3ea2-5abd-b9f4-b090d278c7fb" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521982 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\left(-\\left(x - 1\\right)\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 2\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(z - 2\\right).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,-3,5)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,-3,5).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,-3,5):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)&={f}_{x}(x,y,z)i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)k\\\\&=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\left(-\\left(x - 1\\right)\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 2\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(z - 2\\right))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\left(-\\left(x - 1\\right)\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 2\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(z - 2\\right))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\left(-\\left(x - 1\\right)\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 2\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(z - 2\\right))k\\\\&=\\left(2 x - 2\\right)i+\\left(4 y + 8\\right)j+\\left(-1\\right)k\\\\∇f(0,-3,5)&=\\left(-2\\right)i+\\left(-4\\right)j+\\left(-1\\right)k\\\\&=⟨-2,-4,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,-3,5)$ and passes through the point $(0,-3,5)$ can be written as\n\n$$r=⟨0,-3,5⟩+t\\,∇f(0,-3,5)=⟨0,-3,5⟩+t\\,⟨-2,-4,-1⟩.$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521987 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521991 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521987 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521987 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the level surface $\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 1\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right)=0$ at the point $(0,-2,-3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521987 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8bfc56db-f764-5bb9-b626-8a45b50e648a" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521987 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 1\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(0,-2,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,-2,-3).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,-2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)&={f}_{x}(x,y,z)i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)k\\\\&=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 1\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 1\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2\\left(-\\left(y + 1\\right)\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))k\\\\&=\\left(2 x + 2\\right)i+\\left(4 y + 4\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\∇f(0,-2,-3)&=\\left(2\\right)i+\\left(-4\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\&=⟨2,-4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(0,-2,-3)$ and passes through the point $(0,-2,-3)$ can be written as\n\n$$r=⟨0,-2,-3⟩+t\\,∇f(0,-2,-3)=⟨0,-2,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,-4,1⟩.$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521995 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521967 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521995 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521995 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the level surface $x^{2}+2\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right)=0$ at the point $(1,2,-3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521995 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d14649f6-3c65-5d67-bc1a-d5fd911e8608" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521995 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's set $f(x,y,z)=x^{2}+2\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right).$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=0.$\n\nA normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point $(1,2,-3)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,2,-3).$\n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,2,-3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z)&={f}_{x}(x,y,z)i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)k\\\\&=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2}+2\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2}+2\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x^{2}+2\\left(y - 1\\right)^{2}-\\left(-z\\right))k\\\\&=\\left(2 x\\right)i+\\left(4 y - 4\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\∇f(1,2,-3)&=\\left(2\\right)i+\\left(4\\right)j+\\left(1\\right)k\\\\&=⟨2,4,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the vector equation of the line that is parallel to $∇f(1,2,-3)$ and passes through the point $(1,2,-3)$ can be written as\n\n$$r=⟨1,2,-3⟩+t\\,∇f(1,2,-3)=⟨1,2,-3⟩+t\\,⟨2,4,1⟩.$$\n\nwhere $t\\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521997 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521969 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521997 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186521997 #edb/ref 1071 "Find a vector equation of the normal line to the level surface $\\left(x + 1\\right)^{2}+2y^{2}-\\left(z + 2\\right)=0$ at the point $(0,1,1).$