ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186526584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527121 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fdf514b2-8d3a-5a58-a6b9-5e62c2d37fa5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527143 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527143 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130720" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527143 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527143 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve ${x}^{3}{y}^{2}=2x+y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527143 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28dded9d-d02a-5f94-8a43-d32ef32c8465" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527143 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}{y}^{2} & =2x+y \\\\ {x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-1,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-1,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}{y}^{2}-2x-y)j \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2)\\,i+(2{x}^{3}y-1)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-1,1) & =(3(-1)^{2}(1)^{2}-2)\\,i+(2(-1)^{3}(1)-1)\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i-3\\,j \\\\  & =⟨1,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-1,1)=⟨1,-3⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,-3).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-(-1)}{1} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-3)} \\\\ -3(x+1) & =y-1 \\\\ -3x-3 & =y-1 \\\\ 3x+y+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527163 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186527115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527163 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527163 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527163 #edb/ref 1071 "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527163 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4bf356ec-77aa-55f5-9268-fd51c57a8d9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527163 #edb/ref 1074 "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{y}\\text{ }j+\\frac{1}{z}\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,1) & =\\frac{1}{1}\\,i+\\frac{1}{1}\\,j+\\frac{1}{1}\\,k \\\\  & =i+j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i+j+k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527235 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186527258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527235 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "17075a61-c9d0-5adb-a8b2-23c29d75a027" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527235 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186403468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527240 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186527256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527240 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2598a4b3-3e2b-55cf-8762-35fa24bff20e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527240 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527256 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186527262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527256 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "628d3218-9eb5-5ad9-a24c-b47a38223247" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527256 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759